1
00:00:32,322 --> 00:00:35,123
Vast open plains

2
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:38,526
Immense spaces

3
00:00:39,847 --> 00:00:41,528
Eerie silence

4
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:48,252
But any feeling
of emptiness is an illusion

5
00:00:51,935 --> 00:00:57,659
The plains of our planet support the greatest gatherings of wildlife on earth

6
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:05,051
At the heart of
all that happens here

7
00:01:05,091 --> 00:01:07,852
is a single
living thing

8
00:01:09,254 --> 00:01:10,294
grass

9
00:01:14,457 --> 00:01:16,618
This miraculous plant

10
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:20,581
covers a quarter
of all the lands of the earth

11
00:01:35,731 --> 00:01:37,652
Grasslands exist

12
00:01:37,693 --> 00:01:39,654
wherever there
is a little rain

13
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:42,696
but not enough to
sustain a forest

14
00:01:43,457 --> 00:01:45,458
Some are huge

15
00:01:45,819 --> 00:01:48,101
The Central
Asian steppe alone

16
00:01:48,141 --> 00:01:52,264
extends one third
of the way around our planet

17
00:01:57,882 --> 00:01:59,044
It's summer

18
00:01:59,084 --> 00:02:02,367
and eagles
effortlessly cruise the thermals

19
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:05,890
scanning the
ground beneath for signs of prey

20
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:11,456
In the distant
reaches of Outer Mongolia

21
00:02:11,496 --> 00:02:15,419
one of the planet'
s great migrations is underway

22
00:02:21,913 --> 00:02:26,637
Few people ever
see this extraordinary annual event

23
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:36,929
Mongolian gazelle

24
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:39,613
Two million are
thought to live here

25
00:02:39,653 --> 00:02:41,855
But no one
really knows

26
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:45,861
For much
of the time

27
00:02:45,901 --> 00:02:48,783
they're scattered
through this vast landscape

28
00:02:48,823 --> 00:02:49,864
But once a year

29
00:02:49,904 --> 00:02:51,105
they come together

30
00:02:51,145 --> 00:02:52,666
to have
their young

31
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:58,591
Nearly all will
give birth within the next ten days

32
00:03:03,785 --> 00:03:04,945
Out in the open

33
00:03:04,985 --> 00:03:08,825
communal carving
is the safest way to have young

34
00:03:08,865 --> 00:03:11,305
With so many
pairs of eyes keeping watch

35
00:03:11,345 --> 00:03:15,025
It's almost impossible for predators to sneak up

36
00:03:21,305 --> 00:03:22,625
There are
no bushes

37
00:03:22,665 --> 00:03:23,865
no trees

38
00:03:23,905 --> 00:03:26,545
There's only one
thing to hide behind

39
00:03:27,025 --> 00:03:28,065
Grass

40
00:03:30,453 --> 00:03:33,096
And it's not
very effective

41
00:03:40,829 --> 00:03:46,076
Predators also have a hard time raising their young on open grassland

42
00:03:46,157 --> 00:03:47,399
Without trees

43
00:03:47,439 --> 00:03:50,723
eagles have to
nest directly on the ground

44
00:03:53,924 --> 00:03:56,408
All inhabitants
of the great plains

45
00:03:56,448 --> 00:03:59,051
are exposed
to the elements

46
00:04:17,094 --> 00:04:20,614
Fire sparks
panic in the herd

47
00:04:27,215 --> 00:04:29,255
Gazelles are
born to run

48
00:04:29,295 --> 00:04:32,936
And even young
calves easily outpace the flames

49
00:04:32,976 --> 00:04:35,376
If they can
avoid being trampled

50
00:05:10,424 --> 00:05:12,706
With nothing to
stand in its way

51
00:05:12,746 --> 00:05:16,188
The blaze consumes anything that can't flee

52
00:05:17,446 --> 00:05:19,649
Huge quantities of grass

53
00:05:19,689 --> 00:05:21,051
valuable food

54
00:05:21,091 --> 00:05:22,532
have been lost

55
00:05:26,617 --> 00:05:27,458
And with it

56
00:05:27,498 --> 00:05:29,460
the old
and the weak

57
00:05:35,348 --> 00:05:38,632
The gazelles move
on to new pastures

58
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:41,314
And leave the
desolation behind them

59
00:05:44,619 --> 00:05:48,264
From the ashes
rises the phoenix

60
00:05:48,746 --> 00:05:49,746
grass

61
00:05:49,988 --> 00:05:52,470
the incredible survivor

62
00:05:55,155 --> 00:05:59,442
Because it grows
from a protected part at the base of its stems

63
00:05:59,482 --> 00:06:02,486
grass is almost
indestructible

64
00:06:14,122 --> 00:06:17,326
Able to repair
and reproduce itself rapidly

65
00:06:17,366 --> 00:06:21,249
It covers more of
the Earth's land than any other plant

66
00:06:21,370 --> 00:06:24,694
And feeds more
wildlife than any other

67
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:47,780
Red billed quillia

68
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:52,260
One and a half
billion swarm across the savannahs of Africa

69
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:56,741
These are the
most numerous birds on earth

70
00:07:00,821 --> 00:07:03,301
Some flocks
are so vast

71
00:07:03,341 --> 00:07:07,302
that they can
take five hours to pass overhead

72
00:07:40,947 --> 00:07:45,270
Only grass can
feed plagues of these proportions

73
00:07:45,871 --> 00:07:49,233
The ravenous
hordes devour the seeds

74
00:07:50,234 --> 00:07:55,398
And the leaves and stems are cropped by great herds of antelope

75
00:08:06,466 --> 00:08:12,153
The East African savannahs alone sustain nearly two million wildebeest

76
00:08:25,018 --> 00:08:27,741
They trim the
grass down to its roots

77
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:29,743
leaving little
in their wake

78
00:08:29,783 --> 00:08:32,826
But within days
the plant will recover

79
00:08:32,866 --> 00:08:36,829
and continue to
sustain the biggest herds on earth

80
00:08:48,570 --> 00:08:52,015
Grass is not
confined to the tropics

81
00:08:56,626 --> 00:09:01,674
It manages to grow even in the bitter conditions of the Arctic

82
00:09:09,579 --> 00:09:12,299
Beyond the limits
of the last tree

83
00:09:12,339 --> 00:09:15,780
The planet is
barren and ice locked

84
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:19,821
A frozen no man's
land at the end of the Earth

85
00:09:20,621 --> 00:09:23,102
But for a short
time each year

86
00:09:23,142 --> 00:09:27,343
The long dark
winter releases its grip

87
00:09:38,981 --> 00:09:40,542
Temperatures rise

88
00:09:40,582 --> 00:09:44,623
and grass that has lain dormant and frozen throughout the winter

89
00:09:44,663 --> 00:09:46,863
sprouts once more

90
00:09:51,785 --> 00:09:54,746
Green returns
to the Arctic

91
00:09:56,186 --> 00:09:59,907
The receding ice
reveals an immense flat plain

92
00:09:59,947 --> 00:10:02,028
the size
of Australia

93
00:10:02,148 --> 00:10:05,149
This is the
Arctic tundra

94
00:10:11,790 --> 00:10:13,151
It's a desolate

95
00:10:13,191 --> 00:10:15,112
silent wilderness

96
00:10:15,312 --> 00:10:17,553
but it's
about to change

97
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:36,642
Snow geese

98
00:10:38,892 --> 00:10:41,614
They winter along
the Gulf of Mexico

99
00:10:41,654 --> 00:10:42,615
And in spring

100
00:10:42,655 --> 00:10:45,498
they fly the
entire length of North America

101
00:10:45,538 --> 00:10:47,739
To reach the
Arctic tundra

102
00:10:49,567 --> 00:10:53,566
Five million
birds make this journey every year

103
00:11:10,448 --> 00:11:15,007
Their marathon
migration is almost 3，000 miles long

104
00:11:15,047 --> 00:11:17,567
and has taken
them three months

105
00:11:24,447 --> 00:11:26,567
Exhausted
and starving

106
00:11:26,607 --> 00:11:29,726
they touch down
inside the Arctic Circle

107
00:11:29,766 --> 00:11:32,526
Back at their
traditional breeding grounds

108
00:11:32,566 --> 00:11:33,766
at last

109
00:11:35,712 --> 00:11:38,156
Snow geese
pair for life

110
00:11:38,197 --> 00:11:39,880
As soon as
couples arrive

111
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:43,245
they must stake a
claim to a nesting patch

112
00:11:47,374 --> 00:11:49,779
Ideal sites are
in short supply

113
00:11:49,819 --> 00:11:53,104
And quarrelsome
neighbours are all around

114
00:11:56,789 --> 00:11:59,070
Disputes
can be vicious

115
00:12:33,786 --> 00:12:35,306
Point taken

116
00:12:37,825 --> 00:12:39,505
It's a long
way to travel

117
00:12:39,545 --> 00:12:41,065
But for a
short period

118
00:12:41,105 --> 00:12:44,744
the tundra is the
ideal place for a grazer

119
00:12:44,784 --> 00:12:48,463
The grass grows
vigorously during the short intense summer

120
00:12:48,503 --> 00:12:51,463
And there are
fewer predators than further south

121
00:12:56,172 --> 00:12:56,732
Here

122
00:12:56,772 --> 00:13:00,415
geese can nest on
the ground in relative safety

123
00:13:00,496 --> 00:13:01,297
Nonetheless

124
00:13:01,337 --> 00:13:04,700
this female must
incubate her eggs for three weeks

125
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:06,341
And throughout this time

126
00:13:06,381 --> 00:13:08,423
she will be
very vulnerable

127
00:13:22,929 --> 00:13:25,853
An Arctic fox
surveys the colony

128
00:13:25,893 --> 00:13:28,855
She's been
waiting for the geese all winter

129
00:13:34,742 --> 00:13:37,825
Sneaking up
unnoticed is impossible

130
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:51,042
Perhaps fortune
will favour the bold

131
00:14:06,206 --> 00:14:08,247
The colony is
well defended

132
00:14:08,287 --> 00:14:10,847
There are no
easy pickings here

133
00:14:22,403 --> 00:14:24,966
She's driven
away from every nest

134
00:14:25,006 --> 00:14:27,768
But hunger
compels her to continue

135
00:14:56,601 --> 00:15:00,685
There are more
eggs here than she can possibly eat now

136
00:15:00,846 --> 00:15:03,249
But the nesting
season is short

137
00:15:03,289 --> 00:15:06,813
So she stashes
much of her plunder for later in the year

138
00:15:06,853 --> 00:15:08,894
When all the
geese have gone

139
00:15:21,082 --> 00:15:22,322
Further south

140
00:15:22,362 --> 00:15:23,083
other

141
00:15:23,123 --> 00:15:26,004
bigger predators
prowle the tundra

142
00:15:28,285 --> 00:15:29,406
Wolves

143
00:15:29,766 --> 00:15:34,528
for them finding
food on the plains is an even greater challenge

144
00:15:34,569 --> 00:15:36,410
Not only is
their prey seasonal

145
00:15:36,450 --> 00:15:38,411
it's also
hard to find

146
00:15:38,451 --> 00:15:42,092
They've been
searching for days without a sign

147
00:15:48,938 --> 00:15:52,300
Somewhere in this
immense landscape

148
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:54,140
there is
food for them

149
00:16:01,024 --> 00:16:02,265
This is it

150
00:16:02,545 --> 00:16:03,705
caribou

151
00:16:05,907 --> 00:16:08,108
Travelling
30 miles a day

152
00:16:08,148 --> 00:16:12,870
They can cover
nearly 2000 miles during the summer months

153
00:16:20,875 --> 00:16:24,558
The wolves will
starve if they don't find the caribou

154
00:16:24,759 --> 00:16:27,602
But it's no easy
task to locate prey

155
00:16:27,642 --> 00:16:29,723
That never
stops travelling

156
00:16:50,741 --> 00:16:53,822
Biting flies and
the quest for new pasture

157
00:16:53,862 --> 00:16:56,904
drives the
migration ever onwards

158
00:17:10,118 --> 00:17:37,377
The wolf has
finally picked up the trail

159
00:17:38,978 --> 00:17:41,060
The caribou are close

160
00:17:45,945 --> 00:17:48,026
At last，a chance

161
00:17:51,390 --> 00:17:53,272
The hunt is on

162
00:18:04,708 --> 00:18:06,671
The wolf
panics the herd

163
00:18:06,711 --> 00:18:09,433
and the weak and
young are singled out

164
00:18:18,285 --> 00:18:21,448
A calf is
separated from its mother

165
00:18:59,309 --> 00:19:00,709
At the
goose colony

166
00:19:00,749 --> 00:19:02,190
it's high summer

167
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:04,230
and eggs
are hatching

168
00:19:06,550 --> 00:19:09,431
The young all
emerge within a day or two

169
00:19:09,471 --> 00:19:11,231
A marvel of timing

170
00:19:11,271 --> 00:19:15,351
The colony is now
home to a million goslings

171
00:19:31,861 --> 00:19:35,341
The fox is still
gathering all she can get

172
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:41,420
Sometimes

173
00:19:41,460 --> 00:19:44,300
one mouth
simply isn't enough

174
00:20:20,068 --> 00:20:22,268
One will
have to do

175
00:20:28,613 --> 00:20:30,815
Not all
food is stored

176
00:20:30,855 --> 00:20:33,057
Some is
needed right now

177
00:20:34,939 --> 00:20:37,901
She has seven
hungry cubs to feed

178
00:20:44,717 --> 00:20:46,396
As their
appetites grow

179
00:20:46,436 --> 00:20:49,756
the mother must
work tirelessly to raise her family

180
00:20:49,796 --> 00:20:50,715
Only fat

181
00:20:50,755 --> 00:20:53,995
healthy cubs will
survive the Arctic winter

182
00:21:10,186 --> 00:21:14,230
The vast majority
of the Goslings are still flourishing

183
00:21:14,311 --> 00:21:17,435
Their parents
lead them down to the safety of the water

184
00:21:17,475 --> 00:21:20,358
As soon as they'
re strong enough to make the journey

185
00:21:26,447 --> 00:21:27,809
For the foxes

186
00:21:27,849 --> 00:21:30,371
boom time has
come to an end

187
00:21:30,653 --> 00:21:35,618
But the mother has given her cubs the best possible start in life

188
00:21:38,019 --> 00:21:40,705
The geese will
continue grazing the tundra

189
00:21:40,745 --> 00:21:42,309
until the
summer ends

190
00:21:42,349 --> 00:21:45,435
and they're
forced to head south for the winter

191
00:21:53,792 --> 00:21:55,112
At these latitudes

192
00:21:55,152 --> 00:21:56,991
the sun's
rays are weak

193
00:21:57,031 --> 00:22:00,431
and grass can
only grow here for a few months a year

194
00:22:00,590 --> 00:22:02,150
But further south

195
00:22:02,190 --> 00:22:03,670
summers are longer

196
00:22:03,710 --> 00:22:05,989
and the
grasslands flourish

197
00:22:20,808 --> 00:22:23,651
The prairies
of North America

198
00:22:25,695 --> 00:22:27,538
This rich pasture

199
00:22:27,578 --> 00:22:31,983
once supported
the greatest herds ever seen on our planet

200
00:22:41,999 --> 00:22:45,001
There were once
60 million bison

201
00:22:45,041 --> 00:22:48,803
But no animal is
immune to intensive hunting by man

202
00:22:48,843 --> 00:22:51,324
Or the destruction of its habitat

203
00:22:51,564 --> 00:22:53,205
And a century ago

204
00:22:53,245 --> 00:22:56,727
the bison were
reduced to barely a thousand

205
00:22:57,368 --> 00:22:58,168
Now

206
00:22:58,208 --> 00:23:00,289
thanks to
rigorous protection

207
00:23:00,329 --> 00:23:02,610
the species
is recovering

208
00:23:07,598 --> 00:23:09,721
The growing
season is long

209
00:23:09,761 --> 00:23:13,805
and the grass
here can support herds all year round

210
00:23:19,774 --> 00:23:23,538
Male bison
weigh in at one ton

211
00:23:39,643 --> 00:23:40,525
In high summer

212
00:23:40,565 --> 00:23:43,090
the bulls are fat
from the rich grazing

213
00:23:43,130 --> 00:23:44,734
and in
prime condition

214
00:23:44,774 --> 00:23:46,817
But only a
few will mate

215
00:23:46,898 --> 00:23:48,581
Exactly which few

216
00:23:48,621 --> 00:23:50,584
is about to
be decided

217
00:24:22,894 --> 00:24:25,417
On temperate
plains around the world

218
00:24:25,457 --> 00:24:29,581
Summer is a time
for growth and reproduction

219
00:24:32,385 --> 00:24:35,748
Now the grass
produces its flowers

220
00:24:48,691 --> 00:24:52,373
New colours also
come to the plains

221
00:25:29,949 --> 00:25:35,874
The northern flowering is mirrored by the grasslands of the southern hemisphere

222
00:25:35,954 --> 00:25:41,199
And nowhere is more impressive than on the veldt of South Africa

223
00:26:29,606 --> 00:26:32,289
Not all
temperate plains

224
00:26:32,329 --> 00:26:35,451
are so rich and
colourful in the summer

225
00:26:42,680 --> 00:26:46,439
This is midsummer
on the Tibetan plateau

226
00:26:46,479 --> 00:26:49,558
the highest great
plain in the world

227
00:26:50,998 --> 00:26:52,517
Despite the
conditions

228
00:26:52,557 --> 00:26:55,557
grass survives
and in sufficient quantities

229
00:26:55,597 --> 00:26:59,156
to support the
highest of all grazing herds

230
00:26:59,356 --> 00:27:01,515
those of
the wild yak

231
00:27:04,944 --> 00:27:06,224
Even in summer

232
00:27:06,264 --> 00:27:07,744
life is hard

233
00:27:07,785 --> 00:27:10,385
Temperatures
rarely rise above freezing

234
00:27:10,425 --> 00:27:12,385
and the
air is thin

235
00:27:19,387 --> 00:27:21,947
It's also
exceptionally dry

236
00:27:21,987 --> 00:27:24,667
for one
very big reason

237
00:27:28,068 --> 00:27:29,588
The Himalayas

238
00:27:32,309 --> 00:27:35,509
The great mountain range acts as a barrier

239
00:27:35,549 --> 00:27:38,389
Preventing clouds
moving in from the south

240
00:27:38,429 --> 00:27:41,148
And this casts a
giant rain shadow

241
00:27:41,188 --> 00:27:44,148
That leaves
Tibet high and dry

242
00:28:03,328 --> 00:28:05,571
Grass
clings to life

243
00:28:05,611 --> 00:28:07,732
even as
desiccating winds

244
00:28:07,772 --> 00:28:11,495
remove what
little moisture remains in the soil

245
00:28:20,944 --> 00:28:23,587
So long as
grass can survive

246
00:28:23,627 --> 00:28:25,388
so can grazers

247
00:28:30,697 --> 00:28:32,298
Wild ass

248
00:28:41,470 --> 00:28:44,512
The males are
fighting to win territories

249
00:28:44,553 --> 00:28:46,075
Those that
hold the best

250
00:28:46,115 --> 00:28:49,518
are more likely
to attract a herd of females

251
00:29:05,997 --> 00:29:08,838
It's a
frisky business

252
00:29:13,639 --> 00:29:15,560
That counts
as a victory

253
00:29:15,600 --> 00:29:19,201
But he can't assume the females will actually turn up

254
00:29:24,402 --> 00:29:28,123
Female asses are
mysterious creatures

255
00:29:30,373 --> 00:29:32,576
They come and
go as they please

256
00:29:32,616 --> 00:29:36,059
And much of their
behaviour seems unfathomable

257
00:29:36,099 --> 00:29:37,860
To an outsider

258
00:29:49,544 --> 00:29:52,345
They're the great
nomads of the plateau

259
00:29:52,385 --> 00:29:56,827
And will often trek vast distances across these parched plains

260
00:29:56,867 --> 00:29:58,747
In search of oases

261
00:30:00,548 --> 00:30:02,949
But when they
do find paradise

262
00:30:02,989 --> 00:30:06,510
They are liable
to feed and drink for just a few hours

263
00:30:06,550 --> 00:30:08,631
And then head
back to the dust

264
00:30:08,671 --> 00:30:10,711
For no
apparent reason

265
00:30:15,665 --> 00:30:20,947
Wild ass are the
most conspicuous pioneers of this high frontier

266
00:30:20,987 --> 00:30:25,468
But the most numerous grazer in Tibet lives underground

267
00:30:29,310 --> 00:30:30,270
Pika

268
00:30:30,430 --> 00:30:32,111
a relative
of the rabbit

269
00:30:32,151 --> 00:30:34,391
it too
feeds on grass

270
00:30:40,432 --> 00:30:42,637
On the
exposed plateau

271
00:30:42,677 --> 00:30:45,564
pykhas never
stray far from their burrows

272
00:30:45,604 --> 00:30:46,925
But even so

273
00:30:46,967 --> 00:30:48,931
squatters
will move in

274
00:30:48,971 --> 00:30:50,653
given
half a chance

275
00:31:03,955 --> 00:31:07,956
While groundpeckers and snowfinches can be a nuisance

276
00:31:07,996 --> 00:31:09,437
they're
worth tolerating

277
00:31:09,477 --> 00:31:13,158
because they provide a valuable early warning system

278
00:31:27,310 --> 00:31:29,912
The bizarre
Tibetan fox

279
00:31:29,952 --> 00:31:31,272
The piker'
s nemesis

280
00:31:56,319 --> 00:31:57,600
When stalking

281
00:31:57,640 --> 00:32:00,361
it keeps
below the skyline

282
00:32:00,402 --> 00:32:03,844
perhaps helped by
its curious body shape

283
00:32:04,765 --> 00:32:07,007
But why the
square head？

284
00:33:00,945 --> 00:33:01,945
In summer

285
00:33:01,985 --> 00:33:04,425
the Tibetan
plateau heats up

286
00:33:04,465 --> 00:33:07,545
drawing in warm
wet air from the south

287
00:33:07,825 --> 00:33:10,505
But the water
never arrives

288
00:33:10,705 --> 00:33:12,545
As the moist
air approaches

289
00:33:12,585 --> 00:33:15,025
it's forced
upwards by the Himalayas

290
00:33:15,065 --> 00:33:18,385
And condenses
into huge rain clouds

291
00:33:33,654 --> 00:33:38,856
These clouds drop
all their water on the southern side of the mountains

292
00:33:38,936 --> 00:33:41,777
The very peaks
that keep Tibet dry

293
00:33:41,817 --> 00:33:45,819
are responsible
for the monsoon rains falling farther south

294
00:33:45,859 --> 00:33:48,020
and the
greening of India

295
00:33:58,561 --> 00:33:59,161
Here

296
00:33:59,201 --> 00:34:02,481
soaked by rain
and bathed in tropical sun

297
00:34:02,521 --> 00:34:05,721
grass reaches
its full potential

298
00:34:08,161 --> 00:34:11,961
Elephant grass is
the tallest in the world

299
00:34:24,593 --> 00:34:27,075
Grass that towers
over an elephant

300
00:34:27,115 --> 00:34:30,397
can conceal all
sorts of surprises

301
00:34:39,566 --> 00:34:42,047
The male
lesser florican

302
00:34:42,208 --> 00:34:44,450
It's hard work
getting noticed

303
00:34:44,490 --> 00:34:47,612
when you go
courting in high grass

304
00:35:01,295 --> 00:35:03,978
The long grass
plains of tropical India

305
00:35:04,018 --> 00:35:08,541
are home to some
of the largest grass eating mammals on our planet

306
00:35:08,982 --> 00:35:11,464
And some of
the smallest

307
00:35:13,448 --> 00:35:16,489
Pygmy hogs are no
bigger than rabbits

308
00:35:16,529 --> 00:35:20,692
They're the
tiniest and rarest of all wild pigs

309
00:35:25,615 --> 00:35:28,497
The female is
busy collecting grass

310
00:35:28,537 --> 00:35:30,617
but not for eating

311
00:35:38,718 --> 00:35:40,679
She's
building a nest

312
00:35:55,652 --> 00:35:57,974
Each piglet may
be small enough

313
00:35:58,014 --> 00:36:00,095
to fit in the
palm of a hand

314
00:36:00,216 --> 00:36:02,417
But she
does have ten

315
00:36:16,641 --> 00:36:19,041
This is how
grass can grow

316
00:36:19,081 --> 00:36:22,321
given unlimited
sunshine and water

317
00:36:22,961 --> 00:36:25,962
But on most
tropical plains across our planet

318
00:36:26,002 --> 00:36:29,442
the wet season is
followed by a dry one

319
00:36:39,521 --> 00:36:41,485
On the
African savannahs

320
00:36:41,525 --> 00:36:45,972
grazers are marching in search of grass and water

321
00:37:07,326 --> 00:37:08,488
Without rain

322
00:37:08,528 --> 00:37:11,532
these plains can
become dust bowls

323
00:37:11,572 --> 00:37:14,735
Grass can now
lose its hold

324
00:37:24,707 --> 00:37:27,551
Elephants are in
immediate danger

325
00:37:27,591 --> 00:37:30,594
They must
drink almost daily

326
00:37:36,170 --> 00:37:37,770
Driven on
by thirst

327
00:37:37,810 --> 00:37:41,849
They march hundreds of miles across the parched plains

328
00:38:00,747 --> 00:38:02,468
Relying on memory

329
00:38:02,508 --> 00:38:05,030
the matriarchs
lead their families

330
00:38:05,070 --> 00:38:07,272
to those
special water holes

331
00:38:07,312 --> 00:38:10,034
that saved them
in previous years

332
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:19,481
This one
still has water

333
00:38:19,602 --> 00:38:21,403
But they must
share what remains

334
00:38:21,443 --> 00:38:23,845
with desperately
thirsty animals

335
00:38:23,885 --> 00:38:25,526
of all kinds

336
00:38:43,226 --> 00:38:46,105
These are
tense times

337
00:39:02,771 --> 00:39:05,653
The elephants
dominate the waterhole

338
00:39:05,693 --> 00:39:07,133
But as night falls

339
00:39:07,173 --> 00:39:09,815
the balance of
power will shift

340
00:39:23,603 --> 00:39:26,605
Thirsty herds
continue to arrive

341
00:39:26,645 --> 00:39:28,046
throughout the night

342
00:39:28,086 --> 00:39:30,687
It's a cooler
time to travel

343
00:39:51,688 --> 00:39:52,929
In the darkness

344
00:39:52,969 --> 00:39:54,730
the tables turn

345
00:39:58,655 --> 00:40:02,419
The elephant's
night vision is little better than our own

346
00:40:05,983 --> 00:40:09,187
But lions have
much more sensitive eyes

347
00:40:23,366 --> 00:40:24,766
The cats
are hungry

348
00:40:24,806 --> 00:40:27,367
and the elephants
seem to sense it

349
00:41:08,997 --> 00:41:12,201
Lions don't
usually hunt elephants

350
00:41:12,241 --> 00:41:16,486
But desperate
times require desperate measures

351
00:41:17,168 --> 00:41:19,731
This herd
contains calves

352
00:41:19,852 --> 00:41:21,693
easier targets

353
00:41:26,267 --> 00:41:28,588
But how to
reach them？

354
00:41:44,037 --> 00:41:46,518
The adults
encircle their young

355
00:41:46,558 --> 00:41:49,760
It's an impenetrable wall of grey

356
00:42:18,619 --> 00:42:23,062
A few exhausted
stragglers are still arriving

357
00:42:26,665 --> 00:42:29,107
One of
them is alone

358
00:42:49,537 --> 00:42:53,218
But it's too big
for the lions to tackle

359
00:42:58,621 --> 00:43:01,382
This one looks
a little smaller

360
00:43:14,745 --> 00:43:18,185
A solitary lion
stands no chance

361
00:43:18,905 --> 00:43:21,185
But the whole
pride is here

362
00:43:25,064 --> 00:43:26,424
There are
30 of them

363
00:43:26,464 --> 00:43:29,504
and they're
specialist elephant hunters

364
00:44:37,927 --> 00:44:41,689
This elephant
will feed the whole pride

365
00:44:41,729 --> 00:44:43,450
for at
least a week

366
00:44:53,217 --> 00:44:57,139
Elephants know
these drinking holes are dangerous

367
00:44:57,179 --> 00:44:59,060
But they
have no choice

368
00:44:59,768 --> 00:45:02,049
The dramas
that play out here

369
00:45:02,089 --> 00:45:03,970
are a
savage reminder

370
00:45:04,010 --> 00:45:06,091
of how
important water is

371
00:45:06,131 --> 00:45:08,932
for all life
on these plains

372
00:45:17,937 --> 00:45:21,419
As the dry season
finally draws to a close

373
00:45:21,459 --> 00:45:26,181
Africa's baked
savannahs undergo a radical change

374
00:45:28,337 --> 00:45:31,419
Rain sweeps
across the continent

375
00:45:31,499 --> 00:45:32,380
And grass

376
00:45:32,420 --> 00:45:33,781
the great survivor

377
00:45:33,821 --> 00:45:35,302
Rises again

378
00:45:35,863 --> 00:45:38,224
And the
herds return

379
00:45:40,947 --> 00:45:41,707
For months

380
00:45:41,747 --> 00:45:44,389
they've been
scattered over huge areas

381
00:45:44,429 --> 00:45:48,392
Clinging to
existence around tiny waterholes

382
00:45:48,793 --> 00:45:49,633
Now

383
00:45:49,753 --> 00:45:52,075
the good
times are back

384
00:45:57,831 --> 00:46:01,715
A few African
savannas are very special

385
00:46:01,996 --> 00:46:02,517
Here

386
00:46:02,557 --> 00:46:04,999
rainwater
from far and wide

387
00:46:05,039 --> 00:46:07,802
flows across
the flat plains

388
00:46:08,363 --> 00:46:10,325
Grass is submerged

389
00:46:10,365 --> 00:46:12,567
but still it grows

390
00:46:33,613 --> 00:46:35,335
Flooded， burnt

391
00:46:35,375 --> 00:46:37,617
baked， and frozen

392
00:46:37,657 --> 00:46:40,339
grass can
withstand it all

393
00:46:45,610 --> 00:46:47,850
After six
months of drought

394
00:46:47,890 --> 00:46:51,769
grass replaces
dust over great areas

395
00:47:02,728 --> 00:47:07,407
Fresh new shoots
draw animals from great distances

396
00:47:19,438 --> 00:47:24,362
Many undertake
epic migrations to catch the boom time

397
00:47:32,410 --> 00:47:37,214
Some resourceful
animals live here all the year round

398
00:47:44,828 --> 00:47:47,347
Baboons are
permanent residents

399
00:47:47,387 --> 00:47:49,586
But they have to
change their behaviour

400
00:47:49,626 --> 00:47:51,546
If they're to
gather what they need

401
00:47:51,586 --> 00:47:53,865
On the newly
flooded plains

402
00:47:59,071 --> 00:48:00,870
There's
plenty to eat

403
00:48:00,910 --> 00:48:05,029
But getting to it
can be a little uncomfortable

404
00:48:55,874 --> 00:48:59,833
New water poses
problems for the youngsters

405
00:48:59,873 --> 00:49:03,073
But they know
where to find a dry seat

406
00:49:12,812 --> 00:49:18,095
A juicy snail is
ample recompense for sodden paws

407
00:49:29,662 --> 00:49:32,944
Having survived
the dry barren times

408
00:49:32,984 --> 00:49:36,186
Animals can now
reap the rewards

409
00:49:42,589 --> 00:49:44,509
On this
seasonal planet

410
00:49:44,549 --> 00:49:48,790
the great plains
are lands of feast and famine

411
00:49:49,750 --> 00:49:50,870
At their peak

412
00:49:50,910 --> 00:49:54,031
they support the
greatest gatherings of wildlife

413
00:49:54,071 --> 00:49:56,751
found
anywhere on Earth

